<p style="font-size:.86em;color:var(--ink-soft);margin:1.1rem 0 0;">In a 1978 study (Casscells, Schoenberger &amp; Graboys, <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em>), 60 Harvard medical staff and students were handed essentially this problem. The most common answer was <strong>95%</strong>. The correct answer was about <strong>2%</strong>. Only 11 of 60 got it right. The culprit is mixing up <em>P(positive&nbsp;|&nbsp;sick)</em> with <em>P(sick&nbsp;|&nbsp;positive)</em> — and forgetting that when a disease is rare, the few real cases drown in a sea of false alarms.</p>
